    "content": "# Scuba Diving Age Limit at Netrani Island\n\n**The rule:** to scuba dive with us at Netrani Island, you must be **between 10 and 60 years old** and medically fit.\n\n| | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| Minimum age | 10 years |\n| Maximum age | 60 years |\n| Under 10 | Can come on the boat with a parent and snorkel at the surface. No scuba. |\n| Health | Medically fit, with a medical declaration signed on the dive day |\n| Swimming | Not required |\n\n## Why the minimum age is 10\n\n![PADI certified instructor demonstrating underwater safety hand signal|Every first-time diver receives 1:1 supervision and safety briefing before entering the water](/images/blog/scuba-instructor-hand-signal.jpg)\n\n\n\nBreathing compressed air underwater needs a child who can:\n- understand and follow the instructor's hand signals\n- equalise their ears as they go down\n- stay calm and breathe slowly through a regulator\n- fit the mask and BCD properly\n\nMost children can do this reliably from around 10. Below that, the risk of panic or ear injury is too high for a sea dive.\n\n**Children aged 10–14** dive with their own instructor, just like adults. A parent or guardian must sign the medical declaration and consent form. Kids often turn out to be the calmest divers on the boat.\n\n## Why the upper limit is 60\n\nPressure, cold water and physical effort put extra strain on the heart and lungs. Health risks rise with age, so we set 60 as our upper limit for the try-dive experience. Above 60, enjoy the boat ride and snorkel from the surface instead.\n\n## Health conditions: who should not dive\n\nTell us before you book if you have any of these, and talk to your doctor first:\n\n- Heart disease, or a past heart attack\n- High or uncontrolled blood pressure\n- Asthma or other lung conditions\n- Epilepsy or a history of seizures\n- Diabetes that needs insulin\n- Recent surgery, especially on the ear, chest or abdomen\n- A bad cold, sinus blockage or ear infection on the dive day\n- Pregnancy, at any stage. Pregnant guests should not scuba dive.\n\nYou'll fill a medical declaration on the morning of the dive. If you answer \"yes\" to any condition, we may ask for a doctor's fitness note, or advise you to snorkel instead. This is for your safety. It's not about the money: if you're unfit to dive on the day, talk to us about rescheduling.\n\n## Other common questions\n\n**Can I dive if I wear glasses?**\nYou can't wear spectacles inside a dive mask. Soft contact lenses are fine. With mild short-sightedness most people see well enough, because things look bigger underwater. Read our guide to [scuba diving with glasses, periods and seasickness](/blog/scuba-diving-with-glasses-periods-seasickness).\n\n**Can I dive during my period?**\nYes. Many women dive during their period using a tampon or menstrual cup. If you have strong cramps or feel weak, you can reschedule.\n\n**Is there a weight limit?**\nThere's no strict limit. We need a wetsuit and BCD that fit you properly, so mention your size when you book.\n\n**I'm 58 and healthy. Can I dive?**\nYes, if you're medically fit and below 60. Tell us about any medicines you take.\n\n**My child is 9. Can they try?**\nNot scuba. They can snorkel from the boat with a parent while the rest of the family dives.\n\n## Planning a family dive\n\nFamilies with children aged 10 and above can dive together. Each person has their own instructor. Younger siblings can join the boat and snorkel. For tips on planning the day, read [scuba diving in Murudeshwar with family](/blog/murudeshwar-scuba-diving-with-family).\n\n---\n\nQuestions about whether you can dive? Call **+91 78299 75777** before you book, and we'll talk it through.\n\n👉\n"
