    "content": "# Scuba Diving Price in India (2026): What It Really Costs\n\nHow much does scuba diving cost in India? For a first-time guided dive (a \"try dive\" or \"discover scuba\"), expect to pay **roughly ₹3,000 to ₹7,500 per person**. The price depends mainly on the destination, how long you're underwater, and whether a boat trip is included.\n\nHere's how the main destinations compare, and how to tell a good deal from a cheap one.\n\n## Scuba diving charges by destination\n\n*These are approximate ranges for one beginner try dive, gathered from typical operator prices. They change by operator and season, so always check directly.*\n\n| Destination | Typical try-dive price | Boat needed? | Season |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Netrani Island, Karnataka (from Murudeshwar)** | ₹3,500 – ₹4,500 (our packages) | Yes, about 60–75 min | Oct – May |\n| **Goa (e.g. Grande Island trips)** | Roughly ₹2,500 – ₹4,500 | Yes | Oct – May |\n| **Andaman Islands (Havelock / Neil)** | Roughly ₹3,500 – ₹6,500 | Shore or boat | Most of the year, best Nov – Apr |\n| **Lakshadweep** | Roughly ₹3,000 – ₹6,000, plus travel and permits | Yes | Oct – May |\n| **Pondicherry** | Roughly ₹6,000 – ₹7,500 | Yes | Varies; check with operators |\n\n**Getting there matters too.** Andaman and Lakshadweep usually mean flights and permits, so the dive is cheap compared with the trip. For Bengaluru, Goa and coastal Karnataka, Netrani is reachable by bus or train.\n\n## Why scuba diving prices vary\n\nThe same \"₹3,000 scuba\" can mean very different things. Here's what changes the price:\n\n1. **Time underwater:** 10 minutes and 40 minutes are very different dives.\n2. **Instructor ratio:** 1:1 costs more than one instructor with 3 people, and it's much safer for beginners.\n3. **Boat trip:** an offshore reef needs a boat and fuel. A shore dive doesn't.\n4. **Photos and videos:** included at some places, ₹500–₹2,000 extra at others.\n5. **Dive site:** a real reef versus a shallow sandy patch near the beach.\n6. **Middlemen:** aggregators and hotel desks often add a commission.\n7. **Season and day:** prices can be higher at peak times.\n\n## How to compare scuba diving costs fairly\n\nAsk every operator these seven questions:\n\n| Question | Why it matters |\n|---|---|\n| How many minutes will I be underwater? | It's the main thing you're paying for |\n| Is the instructor with only me? | Safety and comfort |\n| Is the boat included? | It can add a lot if charged separately |\n| Are photos and videos included? | A common hidden extra |\n| Where exactly is the dive site? | Reef or beach |\n| What's the refund policy if the weather cancels? | The open sea is unpredictable |\n| Is the operator licensed and are instructors certified? | Non-negotiable |\n\nThen compare the price **per minute underwater**, not just the headline price.\n\n**Example:** a ₹3,500 dive with 30–40 minutes underwater works out to about ₹90–₹115 per minute. A ₹2,000 dive with 10 minutes works out to ₹200 per minute.\n\n## Netrani Island price in detail\n\nAt Netrani with us:\n\n| Package | Dive time | Price |\n|---|---|---|\n| Full Netrani Dive | 30–40 min | ₹3,500 |\n| Extended Deep Dive | 45–55 min | ₹4,500 |\n\n**Included:** the return boat trip (about 19 km each way), a 1:1 certified instructor, all equipment, snorkelling gear, refreshments, and free HD photos and videos.\n\nThe full breakdown is in our [Murudeshwar scuba diving price guide](/blog/murudeshwar-scuba-diving-price-cost).\n\n## Try dive vs certification: price difference\n\n| Option | What it is | Typical price in India |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Try dive / discover scuba** | A one-time guided dive with an instructor holding you | ₹3,000 – ₹7,500 |\n| **Open Water certification** (PADI or SSI) | A 3–4 day course. After it, you can dive with a buddy, without an instructor | Roughly ₹25,000 – ₹40,000 |\n| **Fun dives (already certified)** | Guided dives for certified divers | Varies by destination |\n\nStart with a try dive. If you love it, consider the certification course.\n\n## Is cheap scuba diving safe?\n\nA low price isn't automatically unsafe, but these are red flags:\n- no briefing before the dive\n- one instructor handling several beginners at once\n- worn-out or ill-fitting gear\n- no medical questions asked\n- pressure to dive in rough sea\n\nRead our [safety guide](/blog/murudeshwar-scuba-diving-safety) for what a safe operator looks like.\n\n## FAQs\n\n**What is the cheapest place for scuba diving in India?**\nGoa and Netrani (Karnataka) tend to be the most affordable when you count travel costs. That's especially true from Bengaluru, Goa or Mangaluru.\n\n**Is scuba diving expensive in India?**\nCompared with many countries, try dives in India are affordable. ₹3,000–₹7,500 is typical.\n\n**Do I need to know swimming?**\nNot for a guided try dive.\n\n**What's the minimum age?**\nAt Netrani it's 10 years, and the maximum is 60.\n\n---\n\nDive Karnataka's best-known reef from ₹3,500: call **+91 78299 75777** or WhatsApp **+91 74063 29777**.\n\n👉\n"
